# The tailnet URL is chosen for reachability, never for origin trust, so the board lands on an insecure context and quietly loses the clipboard

## Goal

Make `switchboard tailnet` emit the **best available origin** rather than merely a reachable
one — preferring a secure context when the tailnet offers it — and, when no secure origin is
available, say in a single line what that costs. Detection only: this plan never configures
`tailscale serve` (see *Non-goals*).

### Problem Analysis

**Measured on the home lab, 2026-09-01** (`patrickremotedev`, standalone
`dist/standalone/cli.js tailnet --no-open`, 14h31m uptime, port 7777):

| probe | result |
| :--- | :--- |
| web UI, on the box | HTTP 200 in **1 ms** |
| API `/catalog`, on the box | HTTP 200 in **7 ms** |
| web UI, from a laptop over the tailnet | HTTP 200 in **180 ms** |
| tailnet RTT to the box | 43 ms avg, **13–88 ms spread**, stddev 28 ms |
| `tailscale status --json` → `CertDomains` | `None` |
| `tailscale serve status` | `No serve config` |

**The reported symptom.** From a laptop browser over the tailnet, the "copy prompt" buttons
and the kanban status messages work most of the time and fail intermittently. The server is
not implicated: it answers in single-digit milliseconds, load average is 0.85, and 5.6 GiB of
15 GiB is free.

**The plumbing is correct — this is not a missing-script bug.** `/terminals?token=…` serves
`sharedDefaults.js`, `clipboardFallback.js` and `transport.js` in that order, so
`injectTransportShim` (`src/services/headlessPanelHtml.ts:74`) is doing its job. And the
fallback is genuinely wired: `window.sbCopyToClipboard` has **28 references** across
`src/webview/`, including four in `kanban.html` and the browser copy path at
`src/webview/transport.js:373`. (The raw `<!-- SHARED_DEFAULTS_SCRIPT -->` marker still
present at `/static/webview/kanban.html` is the un-injected static path, which the shell does
not load. It is not the fault here.)

**Root cause: the origin, not the code.** The board is reached at
`http://<tailnet-ip>:7777`, which is **not** a W3C Potentially Trustworthy origin. So
`navigator.clipboard` is undefined and every copy falls to
`document.execCommand("copy")` (`src/webview/clipboardFallback.js`), which browsers honour
only during transient user activation. The browser copy path is **asynchronous** — the panel
fires a verb, the server returns a `prompt` field, and `transport.js` writes the clipboard
when the response arrives. On a link measured at 43 ms ± 28 ms, some round trips land inside
the activation window and some do not. That is the shape of "usually works, intermittently
breaks".

**The same board on the same server does not have this problem** when opened on the box
itself, because that browser uses `http://switchboard.localhost:7777`. Per
`feature_plan_20260805105305_browser-board-url-defaults-to-switchboard-localhost.md` §1b,
`http://*.localhost:<port>` **is** a Potentially Trustworthy origin, so the real Clipboard API
is available and the fallback never runs. Same code, same server, different origin trust —
and that asymmetry is invisible from the CLI, which prints only an IP.

**A second, independent cost.** `board-installs-to-the-home-screen-as-a-standalone-app.md`
records (research 2026-08-31) that on a remote origin **iOS Safari treats a plain-`http`
manifest as a bookmark and does not apply `display: standalone`** — only `localhost` is
exempt. So the missing secure context also caps the Home Screen install work. Two separate
features are held back by one property of the emitted URL.

**Why the CLI is the right place.** `feature_plan_20260805105305` already built
probe-and-fall-back URL resolution — probe the candidate, use it if it works, fall back if it
does not, log one line naming the reason, and *never emit a URL that failed its own probe*.
That machinery exists and is trusted. The tailnet path simply never received the same
treatment: it has only ever emitted an IP, with no notion that origins differ in trust.

## Metadata

- **Complexity:** 4
- **Tags:** cli, devops, ux, browser
- **Files touched:** `src/standalone/cli.ts`, `src/standalone/bootstrap.ts`, `src/extension.ts`,
  `src/utils/loopbackHostname.ts` (or a sibling resolver module),
  `src/test/tailscale-bind-contract.test.js`
- **Risk:** Low–Medium. The terminal fallback is today's exact behaviour, so the worst
  regression is a slow startup if probes are not capped. A wrong *preference* order, by
  contrast, can log the operator out mid-session (see Edge Case 5).

## User Review Required

None. The scope decision — detect and advise, never configure `tailscale serve` — is made
below and justified; it needs no product call.

## Non-goals

**This plan does not run `tailscale serve`, and that is deliberate:**

- **Privileges.** `tailscale serve` requires root unless the operator is set
  (`tailscale set --operator=$USER`, itself a one-time sudo). A CLI that shells out to an
  interactive sudo prompt cannot run non-interactively and is a bad citizen.
- **Half of it is not automatable anyway.** Enabling HTTPS for a tailnet is an admin-console
  click. No CLI can perform it, so the tool can only ever detect and instruct for that half.
- **Lifecycle Switchboard does not own.** A serve rule persists beyond the Switchboard
  process. Anything that creates one owes a teardown path, and a proxy left pointing at a
  dead port is a worse failure than the one being fixed.

Also out of scope: changing the bind address, the peer check, the `Host` guard, or the CLI
hostname validation. This plan is **exposure-neutral** — it changes which URL is *printed*,
never what is *listening*.

## Proposed Changes

**1. A candidate list for the tailnet URL, highest trust first.**

```
https://<magicdns-fqdn>          ← only if a serve config maps it to THIS port and the cert is live
http://<magicdns-fqdn>:<port>    ← already accepted by the Host guard; better to type than an IP
http://<tailnet-ip>:<port>       ← today's behaviour; terminal fallback, needs no probe
```

Probe each with an HTTP GET to **`/health`** — unauthenticated, and critically it does not
burn the one-time token (`consumeOneTimeToken` returns true exactly once,
`LocalApiServer.ts:310-313`; the localhost plan names this same constraint). First success
wins. Emit exactly one URL.

**2. Read-only detection.** `tailscale status --json` for `MagicDNSSuffix` and `CertDomains`;
`tailscale serve status` for an existing mapping. Tailscale absent, stopped, or erroring is
**not an error** — it falls through to the current behaviour silently.

**3. One advisory line, once, only when the chosen origin is insecure.** It must name the two
concrete costs rather than lecture about TLS: copy buttons fall back to a path that can fail,
and Home Screen install will not launch standalone. One line at launch. Never a repeated
warning, never a banner.

## Composition Roots — both hosts

Per the repo rule, this names both roots and the verification covers both. **The seams each
host wires are the audit, not the verbs each host answers.**

- **`src/standalone/cli.ts` / `src/standalone/bootstrap.ts`** — owns `switchboard tailnet` and
  prints the URL. This is where the resolver is consumed.
- **`src/extension.ts`** — the extension does not bind a tailnet listener, so it may have no
  URL to emit. **Do not assume that.** Diff the two roots by hand and establish whether the
  extension surfaces a tailnet or browser-board URL anywhere (the Connections panel is the
  likely place). If it does, the resolver is shared and **both** roots consume it; if it
  genuinely does not, record that finding in the plan file so the next reader does not
  re-derive it.

This is the one case where the parity rule needs thought rather than reflex: serving is a
standalone concern, but *URL emission* may well be shared, and that is where the split falls.

## Edge-Case & Dependency Audit

1. **Tailscale not installed / not running / `tailscale` not on PATH.** Fall through to the IP.
   No warning — this is the normal case for a non-tailnet launch.
2. **HTTPS enabled but the cert not yet issued.** The first cert provisioning is slow. The
   probe fails, the candidate is dropped, startup is not blocked. Never wait on it.
3. **A serve config exists but proxies a *different* port.** Must not be emitted. Match on the
   target port, not merely on the presence of any serve config.
4. **`Host` guard under a serve proxy.** With `tailscale serve` fronting, requests arrive at
   `127.0.0.1` with `Host` set to the MagicDNS FQDN. `LocalApiServer.ts:7934` records that the
   tailnet bind policy accepts "the tailnet address, the MagicDNS FQDN, and its bare first
   label". **Confirm this by test; do not modify the guard.**
5. **`sb_session` is host-scoped.** It is set with no `Domain`, making it host-only, so moving
   between `100.110.206.86` and the FQDN logs the operator out once. This is why the CLI must
   emit **exactly one** URL and be stable about which — printing alternates invites a
   mid-session hostname switch that reads as a random logout.
6. **Probe cost at startup.** Cap each probe with a short timeout and run at most two (the IP
   fallback needs none — it is today's behaviour). A slow tailnet must never delay board
   launch.
7. **Explicit `--hostname`.** Honoured verbatim: no probe, no substitution, no advisory. An
   explicit user choice outranks the default — the rule already established by
   `feature_plan_20260805105305` item 3.
8. **`.switchboard/api-server-port.txt`** stores only a port and is unaffected. Skills that
   build `http://127.0.0.1:<port>` from it keep working; the bind address does not change.

## Verification Plan

1. **Unit — resolver order.** Candidates are returned highest-trust-first; a failed probe
   removes its candidate; an explicit `--hostname` bypasses the resolver entirely.
2. **Contract — extend `src/test/tailscale-bind-contract.test.js`.** Assert the advisory line
   fires when the chosen origin is insecure and does **not** fire when it is secure. Assert
   `/health` is the probe target, so a future refactor cannot start burning the one-time token.
3. **Standalone UAT (home lab).** With no serve config: the tailnet IP is emitted plus exactly
   one advisory line. Then with `tailscale serve` configured by hand: the `https://` FQDN is
   emitted and no advisory appears.
4. **Extension.** Whatever the composition-root diff establishes — either the extension
   consumes the same resolver and is UAT'd for it, or the plan records that it emits no such
   URL. Not "checked the verbs".
5. **The measurement that settles the hypothesis.** On the HTTPS origin, confirm in the
   browser console that `navigator.clipboard` is defined, and that copy buttons work
   repeatedly with `clipboardFallback.js` never reaching its `execCommand` branch.
6. **Exposure-neutral.** Re-run `loopback-hostname-contract`; a non-loopback peer and a
   non-loopback `Host` must still 403. Nothing about the listening surface changes.

## Open Question — flagged, not hidden

**The intermittency root cause is a hypothesis, not a measurement.** Gesture-expiry on
`execCommand` fits the evidence (async copy path, latency variance, works on a secure origin
on the same box) but has not been observed directly. Verification item 5 settles it.

If copy still fails intermittently on an HTTPS origin, the cause is elsewhere — the most
likely alternative is the `transport.js` WebSocket dropping and taking both the status
messages and the verb responses down together, which would explain both symptoms equally well.
**In that case this plan still stands on the Home Screen install benefit alone, but must stop
claiming the clipboard fix.** Do not let the advisory line's wording outlive the evidence.
